####################################################################
# cat1a_signal_quality.rq — CAT1a condition. HAND-AUTHORED.
#
# NL rule (CAT1): "If a signal supporting a physiological alarm is of
# insufficient quality, the alarm is flagged as likely false positive."
#
# Clause by clause:
#   "a physiological alarm"    — the arriving alarm has category
#                                alarmcat:Physiological. A technical alarm
#                                (e.g. "SpO2 zwak signaal") reports the
#                                quality problem; it is never itself flagged.
#   "a signal supporting [it]" — the signal on the alarm's OWN sensing
#                                pathway: its FunctionalUnit -> sensor ->
#                                signal -> the analysis producing its metric.
#                                Anchored at both ends on the alarm itself
#                                (?fu from its message, ?analysis from its
#                                metric), so a sibling signal on the same
#                                sensor never counts — e.g. an ECG-signal
#                                problem does not flag a respiration-rate
#                                alarm derived from ECG_lead_Impedance.
#   "insufficient quality"     — either any mda:hasQualityState other than
#                                Good currently asserted on that signal, or
#                                the sensor producing it in a fault state:
#                                Disabled, Disconnected or Malfunction — the
#                                signal is not being acquired at all (e.g.
#                                "ECG alle leads los" -> "Asystolie"). The
#                                second branch extends the NL wording
#                                deliberately (agreed 2026-09-21); the NL
#                                table should read "...is of insufficient
#                                quality or not acquired...". Both states are
#                                reported by some alarm on the same pathway,
#                                in practice a technical one. The quality-
#                                state scheme holds only Impaired/
#                                ScalingError today; the Good exclusion keeps
#                                the first branch correct if a healthy state
#                                is ever added. Warning is left out of the
#                                second branch: no sensor alarm asserts it,
#                                and it does not mean the signal is lost.
#   "is"                       — currently: a quality state holds only
#                                while the alarm reporting it is active.
#
# Evaluated once, when the physiological alarm arrives (agreed
# 2026-09-21): the flag informs whether the alarm is annunciated, so a
# quality problem that starts later does not flag an alarm already active.
#
# KNOWN GAP (vocabulary, not this rule): "PHILIPSMONITOR - Resp elektr.los"
# sits on sensor ECG_lead_Resp, but respiration-rate alarms are modelled on
# ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor/ECG_lead_Impedance — so it never reaches any
# alarm's pathway and never flags anything.
#
# GRAPHS. ?gT is the arriving alarm's transient graph (category, message,
# triggeredBy and producesMetric are minted there together); ?gP is a
# persistent graph holding its structural chain (FunctionalUnit, sensor,
# signal, analysis — re-minted per alarm, so any copy carrying these exact
# IRIs is the same pathway); ?gQ is wherever the quality state was
# reported. Grouping the pathway into ?gT/?gP — instead of one free graph
# variable per hop — is what stops hops from being borrowed from another
# alarm's chain.
#
# CONTRACT. Bound on entry: ?alarm (the arriving alarm), ?now.
# Binds: ?evidence (the signal or sensor the verdict rests on) and ?witness
# (the graph that reported it, hence the causing alarm).
# Used by actions/check.rq (the firing log) and actions/flag_insert.rq
# (the stored flag, which keeps the alarm from counting as evidence for a
# clinical event).
#
# VALIDATION — engine/regression.py: cat1a_pos (quality fault, then its
# physiological alarm) and cat1a_sensor_pos (ECG leads off, then asystole)
# flag; cat1a_neg (other device), cat1a_tech (technical alarm on a bad
# signal), cat1a_sibling (respiration-rate alarm while the ECG signal is
# impaired), cat1a_otherfu (SpO2 fault vs heart-rate alarm) and
# cat1a_sensor_neg (SpO2 sensor off vs heart-rate alarm) do not.
####################################################################

PREFIX mda:          <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>
PREFIX mdapoc:       <https://w3id.org/mda/poc#>
PREFIX alarmcat:     <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/alarm-category/>
PREFIX qualitystate: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/quality-state/>
PREFIX opstate:      <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/operation-state/>

GRAPH ?gT { ?alarm mda:hasCategory alarmcat:Physiological .
            ?alarm mda:hasMessage ?msg . ?msg mda:triggeredBy ?fu .
            ?analysis mda:producesMetric ?metric . }
GRAPH ?gP { ?fu mda:hasSensor ?sensor . ?sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal ?signal .
            ?signal mda:analyzedBy ?analysis . }
{
  GRAPH ?gQ { ?signal mda:hasQualityState ?quality }
  FILTER(?quality != qualitystate:Good)
  BIND(?signal AS ?evidence)
} UNION {
  GRAPH ?gQ { ?sensor mda:hasSensorOperationState ?sensorState }
  VALUES ?sensorState { opstate:Disabled opstate:Disconnected opstate:Malfunction }
  BIND(?sensor AS ?evidence)
}
BIND(?gQ AS ?witness)
# validity: every graph must still be in its window at ?now
?gP mdapoc:validUntil ?gP_until . FILTER(?now <= ?gP_until)
?gQ mdapoc:validUntil ?gQ_until . FILTER(?now <= ?gQ_until)
?gT mdapoc:validUntil ?gT_until . FILTER(?now <= ?gT_until)
